      "notes": "Edited only /home/user/idea-to-shipped/content/github-basics/what-git-and-github-are.md. The validator printed 'ok ... final=8 checkpoints=10 sections=12'. `vitest run --project content` reported 'Tests 964 passed (964)', with nothing failing for this lesson. Question ids, the single [x] per question and every Why line are unchanged and still true. I ran no git commands that change anything: the label test was a read-only `git log` in a pseudo-terminal. The gh source files and installer scripts I checked are saved in /tmp/claude-0/-home-user/74a692da-26dd-57ab-b4f9-90621aaa7a8a/scratchpad/verify-wgg/.\n\nTwo issues I found that weren't in the findings. I left both unchanged; they're possible follow-ups:\n(1) The lesson has Windows readers work in Git Bash, and then gives the winget command. GitHub's guide shows that command in a PowerShell block, and git-scm.com says to type its winget command 'in command prompt or Powershell'. Older reports describe winget failing inside Git Bash: 'Permission denied' (microsoft/winget-cli#228) and a prompt that hangs without winpty (#2013). I didn't check whether current Git for Windows still has these problems. A cheap fix would be to say 'in PowerShell' on the winget line.\n(2) Commits and diffs reuses the IDs 9c41d5a, 7b2e8d0 and 5d6c0f2, but with '(#3)'/'(#2)' added to the messages and an extra commit, e2b18c4, between 7b2e8d0 and 5d6c0f2. So the two lessons show the same IDs with different histories, which can't happen, and that lesson teaches readers why."
